  "n17" [label="(31,0,0) Induction-case: By nature of the sequence, any ITR can in theory join any ITR, leading to many permutations that do not all lead to correct split transgene reconstitution, and the approach also currently relies on very high vector doses."];
  "n18" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: When a concatenation-exploiting approach like dual vector has random ITR joining and requires high doses, it has efficiency limitations and unknown mechanisms."];
  "n2" [label="(0,0,0) If the unenveloped 25 nm capsid can package no more than 5 kb of ssDNA into its small lumen, then it imposes a size limit to the coding and regulatory elements that can be included in the transgene cassette, excluding larger genes such as dystrophin or the most accurate Cas9 versions and other base editors."];
  "n20" [label="(33,0,0) Phenomenon: The short fragment of the Alkaline Phosphatase (AP) gene is serendipitously found to be “highly recombinogenic” in the dual vector context."];
  "n21" [label="(0,0,0) Knowledge: If the AP fragment is highly recombinogenic, then it promotes annealing of + and – sense VGs which then become a reconstituted large gene through HR."];
